; pi4FpGate.pi4 - THE DIFFERENTIAL GATE. Run every binary32 operation
;                  through the hardware FPU and through the software core
;                  in math.pi4 and require the two to agree BIT FOR BIT.
;
; DIAGNOSTIC. Does not ship.
;
;   PureMetalForge.exe --compile pi4FpGate.pi4 -t pi4 --load-addr 0x400000 \
;        --stack-addr 0x3000000 --entry-returns -o fpgate.img
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; RUN pi4FpState.pi4 FIRST. THIS IS NOT ADVICE.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; This program executes floating-point instructions. At EL2 those are
; gated by CPTR_EL2.TFP, and nothing in this tree installs an exception
; vector, so on a machine where the trap is closed the first `fmul`
; branches into whatever reset left in VBAR_EL2 and the board goes
; silent. The emitted prologue writes CPTR_EL2 = $33FF to open the gate
; (see EmitCore_A64.pbi's NeedsFloatOps_A64 block), and that write is
; correct only if HCR_EL2.E2H is 0.
;
; pi4FpState.pi4 measures both, prints them, and says in words what they
; mean. It executes no floating-point instruction and cannot hang. It
; costs one run. If this program produces no output at all, that is the
; run to do next, and the answer it gives explains the silence.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHY A SOFTWARE ORACLE IS THE RIGHT ONE, AND WHY IT IS AVAILABLE HERE
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; RaspberryPi4/Lib/math.pi4 carries a complete software binary32 core,
; written in ordinary 64-bit integer arithmetic, because until
; 2026-08-26 this backend refused every floating-point IR node. Its own
; header (math.pi4:78-90) says the core is CORRECTLY ROUNDED and
; therefore "computes the same binary32 an IEEE-754 unit would", and
; instructs whoever lands hardware floats to re-measure against it.
;
; That makes it an oracle of an unusually good kind: it is not a table of
; expected answers that somebody typed, it is an INDEPENDENT
; IMPLEMENTATION of the same specification, running on the same board, in
; the same program, on the same inputs. A table can be wrong in the same
; direction as the code it checks. Two implementations that share no
; instruction cannot be.
;
; THE COMPARISON IS ON BIT PATTERNS AND NOTHING ELSE. Every operation
; here takes two 32-bit integers, hands the same two integers to both
; paths, and compares the two 32-bit integers that come back. No
; floating-point value is ever printed, compared for equality, or used as
; a loop bound. That matters because the interesting inputs are exactly
; the ones ordinary comparison cannot distinguish: +0.0 and -0.0 compare
; equal and are different answers; two NaNs with different payloads
; compare unequal to each other and to themselves.
;
; THE SOFTWARE SIDE USES THE *B ENTRY POINTS, WHICH IS LOAD-BEARING.
; MathFAddB, MathFMulB, MathFDivB, MathFCmpB, MathFFromIntB and
; MathFToIntB take and return raw bit patterns as `.i` integers. They
; contain no `.f` at all, so they still compile to integer code after
; this change and cannot accidentally become a test of the hardware
; against itself. The `.f`-facing wrappers in the same file - MathFAdd
; and friends - would do exactly that and are deliberately not used.
;
; > DO NOT DELETE THE SOFTWARE CORE. math.pi4's own header says the day
; > the backend learns `.f`, everything under THE SOFT BINARY32 CORE
; > should be deleted. That instruction is now half-stale and the half
; > that stands is worth stating precisely: the .f-facing WRAPPERS and
; > the polynomial call sites should indeed go back to operators, and the
; > *B CORE MUST STAY, because it is this gate's oracle. Deleting the
; > reference implementation in the same change that introduces the thing
; > it checks is how a project ends up unable to tell which half is
; > wrong.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT IS COMPARED
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;   add sub mul div      the full N x N cross product of the fixed
;                        vectors, then a pseudo-random sweep
;   < <= > >= = <>       all six relations, full cross product. These are
;                        compared as BOOLEANS, because the hardware path
;                        no longer produces the four-way code the
;                        software MathFCmpB returns - see THE COMPARISON
;                        CONTRACT below.
;   int -> float         over a list of integers chosen for the 2^24 and
;                        2^53 boundaries and the 64-bit rails
;   float -> int         over every fixed vector
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE FIXED VECTORS, AND WHY EACH ONE IS THERE
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Every value the brief for this work named, plus the ones that have
; historically broken binary32 implementations. They are in the
; DataSection at the foot of this file, one per line, each with the
; reason it earns its place. In summary:
;
;   both zeros              +0 and -0 are different bit patterns that
;                           compare equal. Sign-of-zero rules are the
;                           most commonly wrong part of an FP core:
;                           (+0) + (-0) is +0 under round-to-nearest,
;                           but (-0) + (-0) is -0, and 0 * -1 is -0.
;   both infinities         and the operations that must produce a NaN
;                           from them - Inf - Inf, Inf / Inf, 0 * Inf.
;   quiet and signalling    a signalling NaN must be QUIETED on output,
;   NaNs, with payloads     which sets bit 22 and changes the pattern.
;                           A NaN with a payload is the case that tells
;                           propagation apart from default-NaN.
;   the subnormal band      smallest subnormal, largest subnormal, and
;                           the boundary in both directions. Gradual
;                           underflow is the part of IEEE-754 that
;                           hardware most often skips, under the name
;                           flush-to-zero.
;   smallest/largest normal both signs. Overflow to infinity and
;                           underflow into the subnormal band are
;                           reached from here in one operation.
;   the 2^24 cliff          16777216 and its neighbours: the first
;                           integer binary32 cannot represent exactly,
;                           where round-to-nearest-EVEN stops agreeing
;                           with round-half-up.
;   ordinary values         so that a catastrophic failure shows up on
;                           row one rather than only in the corners.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE COMPARISON CONTRACT - read this before believing a comparison row
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; MathFCmpB returns -1, 0, 1 or -2 for unordered. The hardware path
; produces a BOOLEAN per relation, because `fcmp` sets NZCV and the
; emitter branches on one condition. Those are different shapes, so this
; file converts the software code into the same six booleans, using the
; IEEE-754 rule that EVERY ORDERED RELATION IS FALSE WHEN EITHER OPERAND
; IS A NaN, and that `<>` is the single relation that is TRUE.
;
; That conversion is written out in SoftRel below rather than reused from
; MathFLt and friends, and the reason is the whole point of this gate:
; the wrappers in math.pi4 are consumers of MathFCmpB, and the defect
; this gate exists to catch on the hardware side - an inverted condition
; that survived because nothing tested the CONSUMER of a comparison
; result - is a defect of exactly that shape. Two consumers written
; independently is the check. One consumer used twice is not.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DIFFERENCES THAT ARE PREDICTED. REPORT THEM, DO NOT SILENCE THEM.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Two disagreements are expected from reading both implementations, and
; they are written down HERE, before the run, so that finding them is a
; confirmation rather than a discovery and so that nobody is tempted to
; adjust the test until it passes.
;
; 1. NaN PAYLOADS. math.pi4 returns the default NaN $7FC00000 for every
;    NaN input - see the two `ProcedureReturn 2143289344` lines at the
;    head of MathFAddB, and the same in MathFMulB and MathFDivB. The
;    hardware, with FPCR.DN clear, PROPAGATES the input NaN's payload
;    after quieting it; the rule is in the shared pseudocode at
;    Datasheets/arm-a64-instruction-set.txt:505965-505985 (FPProcessNaN)
;    and the default it would substitute instead is built at :505060
;    (FPDefaultNaN), which for binary32 is $7FC00000 - the same constant
;    math.pi4 uses.
;
;    So every row whose operands include a NaN with a payload other than
;    the default should mismatch, and the mismatch should be exactly
;    "hardware kept the payload, software replaced it". Those rows are
;    COUNTED SEPARATELY below so the rest of the result stays readable.
;
;    MEASURED ON SILICON 2026-08-26, so this is no longer a prediction.
;    1136 rows landed in this class, and the hardware's answers are
;    EXACTLY FPProcessNaNs as the pseudocode writes it - confirmed by
;    reproducing the board's hardware checksum, over all 27622 rows, in
;    an independently written oracle. What the A72 actually does:
;
;      - a signalling NaN in either operand beats a quiet one;
;      - within a class, the FIRST operand wins;
;      - a signalling NaN is quieted on the way out by setting bit 22,
;        so $7F800001 comes back as $7FC00001;
;      - the payload AND THE SIGN come from the operand as written.
;
;    That last clause cost the first run its clean sweep, and the fault
;    was in the ORACLE, not the board. It implemented `a - b` as
;    `a + (-b)` and selected the NaN after negating, so a NaN propagated
;    out of the second operand of a subtraction came back with its sign
;    inverted. FPSub selects from op2 as written - the pseudocode is at
;    arm-a64-instruction-set.txt:506956-506988, where FPProcessNaNs is
;    handed op1 and op2 and only the VALUE is negated afterwards. Fixed
;    in tools/a64/a64_f32_gate.py; the corrected oracle reproduces the
;    board's number exactly.
;
;    THE RULING, WITH THE DATA IN HAND. Setting FPCR.DN would make the
;    hardware emit $7FC00000 for every NaN and the two paths would agree
;    on all 27622 rows. THE RECOMMENDATION IS TO LEAVE DN AT 0, and the
;    argument has three parts:
;
;      - FPCR reads $00000000 at reset on this board, so DN = 0 is the
;        state the machine is already in and costs nothing to keep.
;        Setting it needs FPCR's field layout, which is still not on
;        this machine - it would mean fetching Arm DDI 0487 in order to
;        change behaviour nobody has asked for.
;      - Propagation is the IEEE-754 recommendation and what every other
;        AArch64 system does, so a program moving between this bare-metal
;        target and any hosted ARM64 behaves the same. The fleet-
;        consistency argument for DN = 1 only aligns the Pi 4 with the
;        SOFTWARE core, and that core is on its way out of the arithmetic
;        path anyway.
;      - The difference is barely observable. IsNaN() answers the same
;        either way, every domain answer in math.pi4's table (Sqr(-1),
;        Log(-1), Pow(-2, 0.5) and the rest) is produced FROM NON-NaN
;        INPUTS and therefore yields $7FC00000 on both paths, and only a
;        program that feeds a payload-bearing NaN in and reads
;        FloatBits() out can tell.
;
;    WHAT DOES FOLLOW is a documentation obligation rather than a code
;    change: once math.pi4's wrappers go back to operators, a NaN payload
;    survives arithmetic on the Pi 4 and does not on the Picos. That is a
;    real cross-target difference and belongs in the numeric-type notes.
;
; 2. THE NEGATIVE OVERFLOW RAIL OF float -> int. MathFToIntB saturates a
;    too-large negative value at -9223372036854775807 (math.pi4:886-916),
;    which is -(2^63 - 1). The hardware's `fcvtzs` saturates at
;    -9223372036854775808, which is -(2^63). They disagree BY ONE, on
;    that rail only, and only for inputs at or beyond it - which here
;    means -Infinity and the large negative normals.
;
;    Which is right is a question about what the language promises, not
;    about IEEE-754, which does not specify the result of an invalid
;    conversion. The hardware's answer is the conventional one. Reported,
;    counted separately, and left for a ruling.
;
; ANY OTHER DISAGREEMENT IS A REAL BUG in one of the two paths and is
; the most valuable thing this program can produce. Print it, do not
; explain it away.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT THE OUTPUT LOOKS LIKE, AND HOW TO READ IT
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; One line per operation group with a pass count and a mismatch count,
; then up to #FPGATE_MAX_REPORT individual mismatch lines in the form
;
;     MISMATCH mul  a=$XXXXXXXX b=$XXXXXXXX soft=$XXXXXXXX hw=$XXXXXXXX
;
; and finally two checksums. THE CHECKSUMS ARE THE POINT OF THE THIRD
; COLUMN. Printing every one of the tens of thousands of results over a
; 115200 baud line would take longer than the test, so instead each path
; accumulates a rolling 64-bit hash of every result it produced, in a
; fixed order. tools/a64/a64_f32_gate.py reproduces the same vector
; order, the same pseudo-random sequence and the same hash from an
; INDEPENDENT IEEE-754 oracle on the host, and prints the two numbers it
; expects. Three implementations agreeing on one 16-digit number is a
; much stronger statement than two agreeing, and it costs two lines of
; output.
;
; If the hardware checksum matches the host oracle's, every hardware
; result in the run was correct, whatever the software column did.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; COST, AND THE WATCHDOG
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; The software core is slow by construction: math.pi4:114-121 measures
; one MathFMul at 1751 executed instructions. The default vector counts
; below put the run in the region of a few hundred million instructions,
; which is well under a second at 600 MHz, but the watchdog is petted
; inside every loop that can run long because the constants at the top
; are meant to be raised.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

EnableFloatingPoint

XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/uart.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/safety.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/math.pi4"

; The number of fixed vectors in the DataSection at the foot of the file.
; IT MUST MATCH, and nothing can check it for you: the Read cursor would
; simply walk off the end of the table into whatever follows. Counted by
; hand when the table changes, and tools/a64/a64_f32_gate.py re-counts
; the DataSection lines and refuses to run if the two disagree.
#FPGATE_VECTORS = 34

; The pseudo-random sweep. Four operations are run on each pair, so the
; work is four times this number of software operations. Raise it freely;
; the watchdog is petted inside the loop.
#FPGATE_RANDOM_PAIRS = 4000

; The seed. Any value; it is here so that a run is reproducible and so
; that the host oracle can generate the identical sequence.
#FPGATE_SEED = 20260826

; How many individual mismatch lines to print before falling silent and
; only counting. A wall of identical mismatches teaches nothing and takes
; minutes to transmit.
#FPGATE_MAX_REPORT = 24

Global Dim FpV.i(#FPGATE_VECTORS)
Global FpRandState.i

; Counters. Separate ones for the two PREDICTED differences, so that a
; run whose only failures are the known semantic gaps reads as such at a
; glance instead of looking like a catastrophe.
Global FpPass.i
Global FpFail.i
Global FpNanPayload.i
Global FpSatRail.i
Global FpReported.i
Global FpHashSoft.i
Global FpHashHw.i
; THE THIRD HASH, added 2026-08-26 after the first silicon run.
;
; It covers ONLY the rows where the two paths agreed, and it exists
; because the first run exposed a flaw in this file's own verdict logic
; rather than in the compiler. The board reported REAL MISMATCHES: 0 and
; a hardware hash that did not match the host oracle's prediction, and
; the decision table three lines below told the reader that meant the
; lowering was wrong. It did not. The whole difference lived inside the
; 1136 rows already classified as a KNOWN divergence, where the two
; paths are SUPPOSED to differ and the hash therefore carries no
; information about the lowering at all.
;
; A hash over the agreed rows cannot have that problem: every row in it
; is a row where soft and hw produced the same bits, so if it matches
; the host oracle then all three implementations agree on all of it.
; The other two hashes stay, because each still says something the
; agreed hash cannot - see the decision table at the foot of this file.
Global FpHashAgree.i

; ======================================================================
;  THE HARDWARE ARM
; ======================================================================
;  EVERY FLOATING-POINT OPERATOR IN THIS FILE IS IN THIS SECTION AND
;  NOWHERE ELSE. That is deliberate and it is worth preserving: these
;  eleven procedures are the entire surface being tested, so a reader
;  can see all of it at once, and a future change to the lowering has
;  exactly eleven places to be checked against.
;
;  Each one takes bit patterns, converts to `.f` WITHOUT arithmetic via
;  FloatFromBits, applies one operator, and converts back with
;  FloatBits. FloatFromBits and FloatBits go through memory - PokeN and
;  PeekN on a local - so they move bits and never numbers; that is why
;  they are safe to use on a NaN, which any arithmetic would destroy.
;
;  The mask against $FFFFFFFF is not decoration. FloatBits returns a
;  SIGNED 32-bit value by contract (math.pi4:1028-1044), so a negative
;  float comes back as a negative `.i`, while the MathF*B core returns
;  the unsigned pattern 0..4294967295. Comparing the two spellings of
;  the same four bytes without masking would report a mismatch on every
;  negative result in the run.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i HwAdd(ba.i, bb.i)
  Protected x.f
  Protected y.f
  Protected r.f
  x = FloatFromBits(ba)
  y = FloatFromBits(bb)
  r = x + y
  ProcedureReturn FloatBits(r) & $FFFFFFFF
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwSub(ba.i, bb.i)
  Protected x.f
  Protected y.f
  Protected r.f
  x = FloatFromBits(ba)
  y = FloatFromBits(bb)
  r = x - y
  ProcedureReturn FloatBits(r) & $FFFFFFFF
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwMul(ba.i, bb.i)
  Protected x.f
  Protected y.f
  Protected r.f
  x = FloatFromBits(ba)
  y = FloatFromBits(bb)
  r = x * y
  ProcedureReturn FloatBits(r) & $FFFFFFFF
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwDiv(ba.i, bb.i)
  Protected x.f
  Protected y.f
  Protected r.f
  x = FloatFromBits(ba)
  y = FloatFromBits(bb)
  r = x / y
  ProcedureReturn FloatBits(r) & $FFFFFFFF
EndProcedure

; The six relations, each returning 0 or 1. Written as six procedures
; rather than one with a selector because a selector would be another
; place for a relation to be mapped to the wrong operator, which is
; precisely the class of defect being hunted.
Procedure.i HwLt(ba.i, bb.i)
  Protected x.f
  Protected y.f
  x = FloatFromBits(ba)
  y = FloatFromBits(bb)
  If x < y
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwLe(ba.i, bb.i)
  Protected x.f
  Protected y.f
  x = FloatFromBits(ba)
  y = FloatFromBits(bb)
  If x <= y
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwGt(ba.i, bb.i)
  Protected x.f
  Protected y.f
  x = FloatFromBits(ba)
  y = FloatFromBits(bb)
  If x > y
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwGe(ba.i, bb.i)
  Protected x.f
  Protected y.f
  x = FloatFromBits(ba)
  y = FloatFromBits(bb)
  If x >= y
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwEq(ba.i, bb.i)
  Protected x.f
  Protected y.f
  x = FloatFromBits(ba)
  y = FloatFromBits(bb)
  If x = y
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwNe(ba.i, bb.i)
  Protected x.f
  Protected y.f
  x = FloatFromBits(ba)
  y = FloatFromBits(bb)
  If x <> y
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; The two conversions. `x = n` and `n = x` ARE the casts in this
; language - there is no cast syntax - so these two assignments are the
; whole test of scvtf and fcvtzs.
Procedure.i HwI2F(n.i)
  Protected x.f
  Protected m.i
  m = n
  x = m
  ProcedureReturn FloatBits(x) & $FFFFFFFF
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwF2I(b.i)
  Protected x.f
  Protected n.i
  x = FloatFromBits(b)
  n = x
  ProcedureReturn n
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE SOFTWARE ARM
; ======================================================================
;  Thin, because math.pi4 already provides the *B core. Only subtraction
;  and the six relations need anything written here.
; ======================================================================

; There is no MathFSubB. math.pi4's own MathFSub flips the sign bit of
; the subtrahend and adds, which is exact and is correct for a NaN too
; (a NaN with either sign is still a NaN). Reproduced here on the
; PATTERN rather than by calling MathFSub, which takes `.f` and would
; therefore route through the hardware after this change.
Procedure.i SoftSub(ba.i, bb.i)
  ProcedureReturn MathFAddB(ba, bb ! 2147483648)
EndProcedure

; The four-way code turned into the six booleans, by the IEEE rule.
; rel: 0 = lt, 1 = le, 2 = gt, 3 = ge, 4 = eq, 5 = ne.
;
; MathFCmpB answers -2 for unordered. Every ordered relation is FALSE
; against a NaN and `<>` is TRUE, which is the one line of this file
; that a reader should check against the standard rather than against
; the other implementation.
Procedure.i SoftRel(rel.i, ba.i, bb.i)
  Protected c.i
  c = MathFCmpB(ba, bb)
  If c = -2
    If rel = 5
      ProcedureReturn 1
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If rel = 0
    If c = -1 : ProcedureReturn 1 : EndIf
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If rel = 1
    If c = -1 : ProcedureReturn 1 : EndIf
    If c = 0  : ProcedureReturn 1 : EndIf
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If rel = 2
    If c = 1 : ProcedureReturn 1 : EndIf
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If rel = 3
    If c = 1 : ProcedureReturn 1 : EndIf
    If c = 0 : ProcedureReturn 1 : EndIf
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If rel = 4
    If c = 0 : ProcedureReturn 1 : EndIf
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If c <> 0 : ProcedureReturn 1 : EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  OUTPUT HELPERS
; ======================================================================
;  No character literals - house rule. 48 is the code for the digit
;  zero, 55 is the letter A minus ten, 36 is the dollar sign and 32 is
;  a space.
; ======================================================================
Procedure PutHexN(v.i, digits.i)
  Define i.i
  Define n.i
  For i = digits - 1 To 0 Step -1
    n = (v >> (i * 4)) & $F
    If n < 10
      UartWrite(48 + n)
    Else
      UartWrite(55 + n)
    EndIf
  Next
EndProcedure

Procedure PutHex8(v.i)
  UartWrite(36)
  PutHexN(v, 8)
EndProcedure

Procedure PutHex16(v.i)
  UartWrite(36)
  PutHexN(v, 16)
EndProcedure

Procedure PutDec(v.i)
  Define d.i
  Define n.i
  Define started.i
  If v = 0
    UartWrite(48)
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If v < 0
    UartWrite(45)
    v = 0 - v
  EndIf
  d = 1000000000000000000
  While d > 0
    n = (v / d) % 10
    If n <> 0
      started = 1
    EndIf
    If started = 1
      UartWrite(48 + n)
    EndIf
    d = d / 10
  Wend
EndProcedure

Procedure PutSpace()
  UartWrite(32)
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE ROLLING HASH
; ======================================================================
;  h = h * 31 + value, wrapping at 64 bits, over every result in a fixed
;  order. Chosen for one property above all: it is trivially reproducible
;  in another language, so tools/a64/a64_f32_gate.py can compute the
;  expected value from its own oracle without sharing a line of code
;  with this file. A cryptographic hash would be no stronger for this
;  purpose - the adversary is a mistake, not an attacker - and would be
;  another thing to get identical on both sides.
;
;  ORDER IS PART OF THE HASH. Two runs that produce the same multiset of
;  results in a different order hash differently, which is correct: the
;  vector order is part of what is being pinned.
; ======================================================================
Procedure HashSoft(v.i)
  FpHashSoft = FpHashSoft * 31 + v
EndProcedure

Procedure HashHw(v.i)
  FpHashHw = FpHashHw * 31 + v
EndProcedure

; Folded only for a row where the two paths produced identical bits.
; Called from Judge AFTER the equality test, so the ORDER of this hash's
; inputs is the order of the agreeing rows, which the host oracle
; reproduces by applying the same test.
Procedure HashAgree(v.i)
  FpHashAgree = FpHashAgree * 31 + v
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE PSEUDO-RANDOM SOURCE
; ======================================================================
;  A 31-bit linear congruential generator, and the width is the point.
;  The multiply is at most 2^31 x 2^30, which is 2^61 and cannot
;  overflow a signed 64-bit register - so the sequence is exactly
;  reproducible in any language with 64-bit integers and needs no
;  discussion of what this backend does on overflow. A 64-bit generator
;  would have been shorter to write and would have made the host tool
;  depend on wrap semantics that are not written down anywhere.
;
;  Two draws make one 32-bit pattern, taking the middle bits of each,
;  because the low bits of an LCG are notoriously non-random and the
;  point of the sweep is coverage.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i NextRandom32()
  Protected a.i
  Protected b.i
  FpRandState = (FpRandState * 1103515245 + 12345) & $7FFFFFFF
  a = (FpRandState >> 8) & $FFFF
  FpRandState = (FpRandState * 1103515245 + 12345) & $7FFFFFFF
  b = (FpRandState >> 8) & $FFFF
  ProcedureReturn ((a << 16) | b) & $FFFFFFFF
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE ADJUDICATOR
; ======================================================================
;  One place decides pass, fail, or known-difference, so the three
;  counters cannot drift apart and the classification rules are stated
;  once.
;
;  op: 0 add, 1 sub, 2 mul, 3 div, 4 i2f, 5 f2i, 6..11 the relations.
; ======================================================================
; Written as a ladder of plain If blocks rather than the compact
; one-line "If ... : ... : ProcedureReturn : EndIf" form. A bare
; ProcedureReturn after a colon does not parse on this frontend - it is
; read as the start of an expression - which is a small dialect fact
; worth leaving recorded here rather than rediscovering.
Procedure PrintOpName(op.i)
  If op = 0
    UartWriteStr("add")
  ElseIf op = 1
    UartWriteStr("sub")
  ElseIf op = 2
    UartWriteStr("mul")
  ElseIf op = 3
    UartWriteStr("div")
  ElseIf op = 4
    UartWriteStr("i2f")
  ElseIf op = 5
    UartWriteStr("f2i")
  ElseIf op = 6
    UartWriteStr("lt ")
  ElseIf op = 7
    UartWriteStr("le ")
  ElseIf op = 8
    UartWriteStr("gt ")
  ElseIf op = 9
    UartWriteStr("ge ")
  ElseIf op = 10
    UartWriteStr("eq ")
  Else
    UartWriteStr("ne ")
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; Is this pattern a NaN? Answered locally rather than through
; MathFIsNaNB so that the classification of a mismatch does not depend
; on the implementation being judged.
Procedure.i PatIsNan(b.i)
  If ((b >> 23) & 255) <> 255
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If (b & 8388607) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure Judge(op.i, a.i, b.i, soft.i, hw.i)
  Define nanIn.i

  HashSoft(soft)
  HashHw(hw)

  If soft = hw
    FpPass = FpPass + 1
    HashAgree(soft)
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  ; --- predicted difference 1: NaN payload propagation ---------------
  ; Classified, not excused. A row lands here only when an operand was
  ; already a NaN AND both sides produced a NaN - which is the exact
  ; shape of "hardware kept the payload, software substituted the
  ; default". Anything else involving a NaN is a genuine failure and
  ; falls through to the general counter.
  nanIn = 0
  If op < 6
    If PatIsNan(a) = 1 : nanIn = 1 : EndIf
    If PatIsNan(b) = 1 : nanIn = 1 : EndIf
  EndIf
  If nanIn = 1
    If PatIsNan(soft) = 1
      If PatIsNan(hw) = 1
        FpNanPayload = FpNanPayload + 1
        ProcedureReturn
      EndIf
    EndIf
  EndIf

  ; --- predicted difference 2: the negative saturation rail ----------
  ; float -> int only, and only the exact one-apart pair described in
  ; this file's header. Anything else is a real failure.
  If op = 5
    If soft = -9223372036854775807
      If hw = soft - 1
        FpSatRail = FpSatRail + 1
        ProcedureReturn
      EndIf
    EndIf
  EndIf

  FpFail = FpFail + 1
  If FpReported < #FPGATE_MAX_REPORT
    FpReported = FpReported + 1
    UartWriteStr("MISMATCH ")
    PrintOpName(op)
    UartWriteStr(" a=")
    PutHex8(a)
    UartWriteStr(" b=")
    PutHex8(b)
    UartWriteStr(" soft=")
    PutHex16(soft)
    UartWriteStr(" hw=")
    PutHex16(hw)
    UartWriteNl()
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE PHASES
; ======================================================================

; Load the fixed vector table. Read as `.l` because the DataSection
; holds 32-bit patterns, then masked to the unsigned spelling so that
; every comparison downstream is between two values of the same
; signedness. Data.l of $80000000 arrives here as a negative `.l`; the
; mask is what makes it the pattern 2147483648 that the MathF*B core
; expects.
Procedure LoadVectors()
  Define i.i
  Define v.l
  Restore FpGateVectors
  For i = 0 To #FPGATE_VECTORS - 1
    Read.l v
    FpV(i) = v & $FFFFFFFF
  Next
EndProcedure

Procedure RunArithmetic()
  Define i.i
  Define j.i
  Define a.i
  Define b.i
  For i = 0 To #FPGATE_VECTORS - 1
    SafetyWatchdogPet()
    a = FpV(i)
    For j = 0 To #FPGATE_VECTORS - 1
      b = FpV(j)
      Judge(0, a, b, MathFAddB(a, b), HwAdd(a, b))
      Judge(1, a, b, SoftSub(a, b),   HwSub(a, b))
      Judge(2, a, b, MathFMulB(a, b), HwMul(a, b))
      Judge(3, a, b, MathFDivB(a, b), HwDiv(a, b))
    Next
  Next
EndProcedure

Procedure RunRelations()
  Define i.i
  Define j.i
  Define a.i
  Define b.i
  For i = 0 To #FPGATE_VECTORS - 1
    SafetyWatchdogPet()
    a = FpV(i)
    For j = 0 To #FPGATE_VECTORS - 1
      b = FpV(j)
      Judge(6,  a, b, SoftRel(0, a, b), HwLt(a, b))
      Judge(7,  a, b, SoftRel(1, a, b), HwLe(a, b))
      Judge(8,  a, b, SoftRel(2, a, b), HwGt(a, b))
      Judge(9,  a, b, SoftRel(3, a, b), HwGe(a, b))
      Judge(10, a, b, SoftRel(4, a, b), HwEq(a, b))
      Judge(11, a, b, SoftRel(5, a, b), HwNe(a, b))
    Next
  Next
EndProcedure

; The integers converted to float. Chosen for the boundaries that matter
; on a 64-bit target: the 2^24 cliff where binary32 stops being able to
; represent consecutive integers, the ties that round-to-even decides,
; and both 64-bit rails - including INT64_MIN, which is the one input
; MathFFromIntB cannot negate (`0 - n` overflows back to itself) and
; where a disagreement would therefore be a genuine software bug rather
; than a hardware one.
Procedure RunI2F()
  Define i.i
  Define n.i
  Restore FpGateInts
  For i = 0 To 27
    Read.q n
    Judge(4, n & $FFFFFFFF, (n >> 32) & $FFFFFFFF, MathFFromIntB(n), HwI2F(n))
  Next
EndProcedure

Procedure RunF2I()
  Define i.i
  Define a.i
  For i = 0 To #FPGATE_VECTORS - 1
    a = FpV(i)
    Judge(5, a, 0, MathFToIntB(a), HwF2I(a))
  Next
EndProcedure

Procedure RunRandom()
  Define k.i
  Define a.i
  Define b.i
  FpRandState = #FPGATE_SEED
  For k = 1 To #FPGATE_RANDOM_PAIRS
    If (k % 64) = 0
      SafetyWatchdogPet()
    EndIf
    a = NextRandom32()
    b = NextRandom32()
    Judge(0, a, b, MathFAddB(a, b), HwAdd(a, b))
    Judge(1, a, b, SoftSub(a, b),   HwSub(a, b))
    Judge(2, a, b, MathFMulB(a, b), HwMul(a, b))
    Judge(3, a, b, MathFDivB(a, b), HwDiv(a, b))
  Next
EndProcedure

Procedure Line(*label, v.i)
  UartWriteStr(*label)
  PutDec(v)
  UartWriteNl()
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Main()
  UartInit(115200)

  ; Sixty seconds. Generous, because the constants at the top are meant
  ; to be raised and a watchdog that fires because somebody asked for
  ; more vectors teaches the wrong lesson. Petted inside every loop.
  SafetyWatchdogStart(60)

  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteStr("pi4FpGate - hardware FPU against the software core in math.pi4")
  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteStr("If nothing follows this line, run pi4FpState.pi4: FP is trapped.")
  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteNl()

  LoadVectors()

  UartWriteStr("phase 1: arithmetic, full cross product")
  UartWriteNl()
  RunArithmetic()

  UartWriteStr("phase 2: the six relations, full cross product")
  UartWriteNl()
  RunRelations()

  UartWriteStr("phase 3: integer to float")
  UartWriteNl()
  RunI2F()

  UartWriteStr("phase 4: float to integer")
  UartWriteNl()
  RunF2I()

  UartWriteStr("phase 5: pseudo-random sweep")
  UartWriteNl()
  RunRandom()

  UartWriteNl()
  Line("agreed bit for bit : ", FpPass)
  Line("REAL MISMATCHES    : ", FpFail)
  Line("NaN payload rows   : ", FpNanPayload)
  Line("f2i rail rows      : ", FpSatRail)
  UartWriteNl()

  UartWriteStr("hash agree = ")
  PutHex16(FpHashAgree)
  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteStr("hash soft  = ")
  PutHex16(FpHashSoft)
  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteStr("hash hw    = ")
  PutHex16(FpHashHw)
  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteStr("Compare all three against: python tools/a64/a64_f32_gate.py")
  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteNl()

  ; --- THE VERDICT READS THE COUNTERS FIRST, AND THAT IS THE FIX -----
  ;
  ; The first silicon run, 2026-08-26, printed REAL MISMATCHES: 0 and a
  ; hardware hash that did not match the host oracle. The verdict this
  ; block used to print keyed off the hashes and would have told the
  ; reader the lowering was broken, three lines under a counter saying
  ; nothing was broken. Two statements in one screen of output
  ; contradicting each other, and the false one louder.
  ;
  ; THE ORDERING RULE THAT PREVENTS IT: FpFail is the only thing that
  ; can say "something is wrong", because it is the only thing computed
  ; per row from a direct comparison of the two paths. A hash can differ
  ; for a reason the row-level classification has ALREADY accepted - a
  ; NaN payload is the case in point - so a hash may narrow down where a
  ; difference lives but must never be the thing that declares one.
  ;
  ; (For the record, the run that taught this: the oracle was wrong, not
  ; the board. It flipped a NaN's sign bit when predicting subtraction,
  ; because it implemented `a - b` as `a + (-b)` and negated BEFORE
  ; selecting the NaN, while FPSub selects from op2 as written. Fixed;
  ; the corrected oracle reproduces the board's hardware hash exactly.)
  If FpFail > 0
    UartWriteStr("VERDICT: THERE ARE REAL MISMATCHES. One of the two paths on")
    UartWriteNl()
    UartWriteStr("         this board is wrong - the rows printed above are")
    UartWriteNl()
    UartWriteStr("         outside every known divergence. Report them")
    UartWriteNl()
    UartWriteStr("         verbatim. Do not change this test.")
    UartWriteNl()
  Else
    UartWriteStr("VERDICT: NO REAL MISMATCH. Every row either agreed bit for")
    UartWriteNl()
    UartWriteStr("         bit or fell into one of the two divergences this")
    UartWriteNl()
    UartWriteStr("         file predicted in writing beforehand, both of")
    UartWriteNl()
    UartWriteStr("         which are semantic rulings and not defects.")
    UartWriteNl()
    UartWriteNl()
    UartWriteStr("         Now check the hashes, in this order:")
    UartWriteNl()
    UartWriteStr("         AGREE matches -> all three implementations agree")
    UartWriteNl()
    UartWriteStr("           on every row where agreement was expected. This")
    UartWriteNl()
    UartWriteStr("           is the line that clears the lowering.")
    UartWriteNl()
    UartWriteStr("         AGREE differs -> the encodings or the lowering ARE")
    UartWriteNl()
    UartWriteStr("           suspect. Run tools/a64/a64_fp_check.py first.")
    UartWriteNl()
    UartWriteStr("         SOFT differs -> math.pi4's core has changed or has")
    UartWriteNl()
    UartWriteStr("           a bug; the hardware is not implicated.")
    UartWriteNl()
    UartWriteStr("         HW differs while AGREE matches -> the difference is")
    UartWriteNl()
    UartWriteStr("           confined to NaN PAYLOAD VALUES. The hardware is")
    UartWriteNl()
    UartWriteStr("           producing a NaN; the host oracle predicted a")
    UartWriteNl()
    UartWriteStr("           different one. That is a model question, NOT a")
    UartWriteNl()
    UartWriteStr("           lowering defect.")
  EndIf
  UartWriteNl()

  UartDrain()

  ; The return value the monitor prints: the count of REAL mismatches,
  ; so a run whose console scrolled past is still readable from one
  ; number. Zero is the good answer.
  ProcedureReturn FpFail
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE FIXED VECTORS
; ======================================================================
;  #FPGATE_VECTORS must equal the count of values here. Counted by hand;
;  tools/a64/a64_f32_gate.py re-counts them and refuses to run if the
;  constant disagrees.
;
;  ORDER IS PART OF THE CONTRACT because the checksums are order
;  dependent. Add new vectors at the END and raise the constant; do not
;  insert into the middle unless you are also regenerating the expected
;  hashes.
;
;  > THE COUNT MUST STAY A MULTIPLE OF TWO, AND HERE IS WHY.
;  >
;  > The emitter lays both DataSections down consecutively after one
;  > `.align 8` (EmitCore_A64.pbi's dataOut block, which explains that a
;  > doubleword `ldr` on Device memory FAULTS if it is not naturally
;  > aligned - and with the MMU off, all of DRAM is Device memory).
;  >
;  > This table is 4 bytes per entry and the NEXT table is read with
;  > `Read.q`, eight bytes at a time. So the second table starts at
;  > 4 x (number of vectors) bytes past an 8-aligned base, and it is
;  > 8-aligned only when that count is EVEN.
;  >
;  > At 34 vectors it is 136 bytes, which is 17 x 8, and everything is
;  > fine. At 35 it would be 140, the first `Read.q` of FpGateInts would
;  > be a misaligned doubleword load, and the board would take a data
;  > abort into an exception vector nothing in this tree installs - a
;  > silent hang, from adding one test vector.
;  >
;  > Verified for the current table by reading the emitted assembly on
;  > 2026-08-26 rather than by trusting the arithmetic. If you add an odd
;  > number of vectors, add a padding one as well.
; ======================================================================
DataSection
FpGateVectors:
  ; the two zeros - different patterns that compare equal
  Data.l $00000000       ; +0.0
  Data.l $80000000       ; -0.0
  ; the smallest and largest subnormals, both signs. Gradual underflow
  ; is what flush-to-zero silently discards.
  Data.l $00000001       ; smallest positive subnormal, 2^-149
  Data.l $80000001       ; smallest negative subnormal
  Data.l $007FFFFF       ; largest positive subnormal
  Data.l $807FFFFF       ; largest negative subnormal
  Data.l $00400000       ; a subnormal in the middle of the band
  ; the normal boundary in both directions
  Data.l $00800000       ; smallest positive normal, 2^-126
  Data.l $80800000       ; smallest negative normal
  Data.l $7F7FFFFF       ; largest finite, about 3.4028235e38
  Data.l $FF7FFFFF       ; most negative finite
  ; the infinities
  Data.l $7F800000       ; +Infinity
  Data.l $FF800000       ; -Infinity
  ; NaNs. The default, a quiet NaN with a payload, a signalling NaN, and
  ; a negative NaN. The payload cases are the ones that tell propagation
  ; apart from default-NaN substitution.
  Data.l $7FC00000       ; the default quiet NaN
  Data.l $7FC12345       ; a quiet NaN with a payload
  Data.l $FFC00000       ; a negative quiet NaN
  Data.l $7F800001       ; a signalling NaN - must be quieted on output
  Data.l $7FBFFFFF       ; the largest signalling NaN
  ; ordinary values, so a total failure is visible on the first rows
  Data.l $3F800000       ; 1.0
  Data.l $BF800000       ; -1.0
  Data.l $40000000       ; 2.0
  Data.l $3F000000       ; 0.5
  Data.l $40400000       ; 3.0
  Data.l $40E00000       ; 7.0
  Data.l $C0490FDB       ; -pi
  ; the 2^24 cliff, where binary32 stops representing consecutive
  ; integers and round-to-nearest-EVEN parts company with round-half-up
  Data.l $4B7FFFFF       ; 16777215, the last exactly representable one
  Data.l $4B800000       ; 16777216 = 2^24
  Data.l $4B800001       ; 16777218 - the next representable, not 17
  Data.l $4B800002       ; 16777220
  ; values engineered to land on a rounding tie when added or multiplied
  Data.l $3F800001       ; 1.0 plus one ulp
  Data.l $33800000       ; 2^-24, the ulp of 1.0 halved - 1.0 + this is a tie
  Data.l $34000000       ; 2^-23, the ulp of 1.0
  ; the 2^63 and 2^31 neighbourhoods, which is where float -> int
  ; saturates and where the two implementations are predicted to differ
  Data.l $5F000000       ; 2^63 exactly - the first value past INT64_MAX
  Data.l $4F000000       ; 2^31 exactly
EndDataSection

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The integers for the int -> float direction. Twenty-eight of them;
;  RunI2F's loop bound must match.
;
;  Data.q, not Data.l: `.i` is 64 bits on this target and the whole
;  question this phase answers is what happens to the bits above 32.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
DataSection
FpGateInts:
  Data.q 0
  Data.q 1
  Data.q -1
  Data.q 2
  Data.q -2
  Data.q 16777215            ; 2^24 - 1, the last exact one
  Data.q 16777216            ; 2^24
  Data.q 16777217            ; 2^24 + 1 - NOT representable, rounds
  Data.q 16777218            ; 2^24 + 2 - representable again
  Data.q 16777219            ; rounds, and the tie goes to even
  Data.q -16777217
  Data.q 2147483647          ; INT32_MAX
  Data.q -2147483648         ; INT32_MIN
  Data.q 2147483648          ; one past INT32_MAX - a 32-bit conversion
                             ; would wrap here and a 64-bit one will not
  Data.q 4294967295          ; UINT32_MAX
  Data.q 4294967296          ; 2^32
  Data.q 1099511627775
  Data.q 9007199254740993    ; 2^53 + 1
  Data.q 123456789
  Data.q -123456789
  Data.q 1000000000000
  Data.q -1000000000000
  Data.q 9223372036854775807 ; INT64_MAX
  Data.q -9223372036854775807
  ; INT64_MIN. MathFFromIntB negates a negative input with `0 - n`,
  ; which for this value overflows back to itself, so the software path
  ; may be wrong here. That is worth knowing and is exactly why it is in
  ; the list rather than left out for being awkward.
  Data.q -9223372036854775808
  Data.q 4611686018427387904 ; 2^62
  Data.q 6917529027641081856 ; 2^62 + 2^61, a tie at the top of the range
  Data.q -4611686018427387904
EndDataSection
